in @prism

in @optics
in @glass
in @nl
in @mirror/lens
in @mirror/lens/cli

# @mirror/lens/cli/compile — the compile stage.
#
# First of eight verb sub-stages forward-promised in
# docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3 (table at §3 + §3.1 shape sketch).
# Realizes the `command compile` declaration in mirror.spec at the
# stage altitude — the substrate level where the named loop
# (focus -> project -> shift -> settle) is declared, walked, and
# discharged.
#
# === Why compile is a stage, not an op ===
#
# Per cli-as-prism §2.1: compile is A NAMED LOOP, not a single op.
# Top-level `mirror settle` is the algebraic operation (one write,
# one proof, no loop). `compile` is the canonical pipeline given a
# name because users say "build" more often than they say "settle
# store after shift @facet/rust". The stage's `settle` IS the build;
# the stage's `focus` previews what would change.
#
# Per optical-keywords.md §1.2: a `stage` is a single-input,
# single-output chain of facets that substitutes as a facet at the
# next altitude up. The compile stage substitutes as a facet of the
# parent bench (@mirror/lens/cli); its produced prism exposes the
# same five operations on the compile sub-manifold (the grammar
# directory under settlement).
#
# === Family placement ===
#
# Parent bench: @mirror/lens/cli (shards/mirror/lens/cli.mirror).
# Family root:  @mirror/lens    (shards/mirror/lens.mirror).
# Sibling stages (forward-promised, not yet minted):
#   @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi   — the coherence-settling loop
#   @mirror/lens/cli/shatter    — materialize AST -> text
#   @mirror/lens/cli/bootstrap  — autopoietic phase machine
#   @mirror/lens/cli/join       — peer-entry (Glanville's verb)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/reflect    — VSM System 5 view
#   @mirror/lens/cli/time       — Elm-style time travel
#   @mirror/lens/cli/crack      — third-state lifecycle
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/mirror/lens/cli/compile.mirror` declares
# `@mirror/lens/cli/compile` and only that.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3, §3.1 (shape sketch),
#               §4.5 (compile vs top-level settle), §7 (default-op
#               rule: compile declares `default settle`),
#               docs/specs/optical-keywords.md §1.2 (stage),
#               mirror.spec (`command compile { arg path: ~d;
#               flag strict: bool = true }`).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]
#                      (stage IS a sub-prism specialization),
#                      [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]
#                      (file path matches namespace),
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (shift = basis transformation; same content,
#                      different altitude).
# Related shards: lens/cli.mirror (parent bench, the produced-prism
#                 form the user types),
#                 mirror/mosaic.mirror (the build altitude this stage
#                 ultimately dispatches into — `settle(emitter)` is
#                 what `compile.settle` walks to),
#                 io/cargo.mirror (the @io boundary where the
#                 produced binary lands when altitude=@facet/rust),
#                 glass.mirror (imperfect / transparency carriers).

stage @mirror/lens/cli/compile {
  # Per cli-as-prism §7: compile declares `default settle` — the
  # canonical safe-write default. Bare `mirror compile T` runs the
  # build (settles the grammar at T); the stage name alone is
  # enough because the act is a side-effect-bounded artifact write.
  # The other six stages (kintsugi, bootstrap, join, reflect, time,
  # crack) inherit `default focus`; `shatter` is the only sibling
  # that also declares `default settle`.
  default settle

  # The five operations the produced prism exposes on the compile
  # sub-manifold (a grammar directory under settlement). Each operand
  # below names the natural shape of the manifold's element at that
  # op's slot, per the spec §3.1 sketch convention:
  #   focus    target     — peek what would compile (no write)
  #   project  predicate  — filter the grammar set by a predicate
  #   split    candidate  — enumerate build candidates / import graph
  #   shift    altitude   — view the same grammar at a different
  #                          declared altitude (e.g. @facet/rust ->
  #                          @meta/ast); shift is basis transform,
  #                          per [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
  #   settle   target     — the ONE write; runs the build, emits the
  #                          artifact, produces a proof block
  focus    target
  project  predicate
  split    candidate
  shift    altitude
  settle   target
}

# === Compile-specific actions ===
#
# Realizes mirror.spec's `command compile { arg path: ~d;
# flag strict: bool = true }` at the stage altitude. The action
# signature carries the same shape — a grammar-directory ref plus
# the strict-mode flag — at the substrate altitude so the dispatcher
# can walk from cli surface to settled call without leaving the
# substrate. The Rust runtime walks these declarations; it does not
# name commands. Substrate-pull discipline per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
#
# Honest `partials` from birth (per recognition #306): the return
# type is the substrate's `imperfect` functor (declared in
# shards/glass.mirror as `imperfect(a, e, l) = success(a) |
# partial(a, l) | failure(e, l)`). A partial compile carries the
# transparency-tagged loss so downstream consumers (mosaic,
# kintsugi, eigenboard) can read the residual opacity without
# pretending the build succeeded cleanly.

# compile(path) — settle the grammar at `path` in default strict
# mode (strict=true). Returns the imperfect carrying the produced
# artifact ref on success, the partial-with-loss on tolerable
# strict-mode misses, the failure with reason on hard error.
compile(path: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# compile_strict(path, strict) — same as compile but with explicit
# strict-mode override. `strict=true` matches mirror.spec's default
# (hard-fail on Dark / unrecognized AST nodes); `strict=false`
# tolerates Dark regions and lifts them as partials carrying the
# transparency-tagged opacity map. Per
# [[feedback-manual-closure-is-training-pull]]: Dark regions are
# kintsugi's mutation targets, NOT manual closure tasks; the
# non-strict mode preserves them as partials for the kintsugi loop
# to consume rather than patching them with opaque carriers.
compile_strict(path: ref, strict: bool) -> imperfect { \ }

out @mirror/lens/cli/compile
out compile
out compile_strict
